---
import "../../../styles/by-election-details.css";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MiniMap from "../../../components/MiniMap.astro";
import PartyBars from "../../../components/PartyBars.astro";
import { partyColour } from "../../../lib/predictions";
import { shortPartyLabel } from "../../../lib/siteData";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";

const data = JSON.parse(
  readFileSync(
    path.join(process.cwd(), "data/predictions/by-elections/clacton-2026-08-13.json"),
    "utf8",
  ),
);

const contest = data.contest;
const prior = data.prior_result;
const priorRows = Object.entries(prior.shares as Record<string, number>)
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
  .map(([party, share]) => ({ party, pct: share }));

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Contest state. "upcoming" is the pre-poll page. Setting status to
// "polls_closed" (22:00 BST on polling day, not before) promotes the embargoed
// winner call in data.call. Setting it to "concluded" additionally renders the
// declared result, which degrades gracefully until the returning officer's
// per-candidate breakdown is backfilled into result.shares.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
const status = (data.status as string) || "upcoming";
const call = (data.call as any) || null;
const showCall = Boolean(call) && (status === "polls_closed" || status === "concluded");
const result = (data.result as any) || null;
const declared = status === "concluded" && Boolean(result?.declared);
const resultShares = (result?.shares as Record<string, number> | null) || null;
const resultRows = resultShares
  ? Object.entries(resultShares)
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .map(([party, share]) => ({ party, pct: share }))
  : [];
const pct1 = (x: number | null | undefined) =>
  typeof x === "number" ? (x * 100).toFixed(1) : null;

const cPolling = (data.constituency_polling as any) || null;
const cPolls = (cPolling?.polls as Array<any>) || [];
const exitPoll = (data.exit_poll as any) || null;
const pollRows = (p: any) =>
  Object.entries(p.shares as Record<string, number>)
    .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
    .map(([party, share]) => ({ party, pct: share }));

const timeline: Array<{ date: string; event: string }> = [
  { date: "7 July 2026", event: "Nigel Farage announces he will vacate the seat and stand in the resulting by-election." },
  { date: "8 July 2026", event: "Resignation effected (appointment as Steward and Bailiff of the Manor of Northstead)." },
  { date: "9 July 2026", event: "Writ moved; polling day set for 13 August." },
  { date: "17 July 2026", event: "Nominations close on a record field of 34 candidates." },
  { date: "28 July 2026", event: "Voter registration deadline." },
  { date: "13 August 2026", event: "Polling day. Polls closed at 22:00 and the contest was called: Reform UK hold." },
  { date: "14 August 2026", event: "Result declared by the Acting Returning Officer. Farage returned with 22,239 votes (63.3%), a majority of 12,784 over Count Binface." },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  breadcrumbs={[{name:"By-elections",href:"/by-elections/"},{name:"Clacton",href:"/by-elections/clacton/"}]}
  title="Clacton by-election result, 13 August 2026"
  description="Clacton result, 13 August 2026: Nigel Farage elected with 22,239 votes and a majority of 12,784. Full results for all 34 candidates and official sources."
>
  <section class="page-hero compact-hero">
    {contest.pcon24cd && (
      <div class="mini-map-side">
        <MiniMap
          focalCode={contest.pcon24cd}
          focalKind="pcon"
          focalColour={partyColour("Reform UK")}
          width={240}
          height={170}
          ariaLabel="Location map of Clacton on the UK map"
        />
      </div>
    )}
    <p class="eyebrow">By-election · Thursday 13 August 2026 · declared</p>
    <h1>Clacton by-election result</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Nigel Farage resigned as MP for Clacton on 8 July 2026, amid a parliamentary standards
      investigation into his financial interests, in order to trigger a by-election and stand in it
      himself. Labour, the Conservatives, the Liberal Democrats, the Greens and Restore Britain all
      declined to contest it. He was returned on 13 August with 63.3% of the vote, on a turnout of
      44.4%, against a record field of 34 candidates.
    </p>
    <p class="meta-row">England · East of England · GE2024: Reform UK, majority 8,405 (18.3pp)</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="detail-nav" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#result">Result</a><a href="#candidate-results">All candidate results</a><a href="#archive">Archived analysis</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav>
  {declared && (
    <section class="section" id="result">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Declared result · 13 August 2026</p>
        <h2>{result.outcome || call?.outcome}</h2>
      </div>
      {resultRows.length ? (
        <PartyBars large={true} rows={resultRows} />
      ) : (
        <p class="caption">
          Declared by {result.returning_officer}. The full per-candidate vote breakdown appears
          here once the returning officer publishes it.
        </p>
      )}
      <p class="caption">
        Declared by {result.returning_officer} on 14 August 2026, from a record field of{" "}
        {result.candidate_count} candidates. {result.denominator_note}
      </p>
      <p class="caption">
        {pct1(result.turnout_pct) && <>Turnout {pct1(result.turnout_pct)}%</>}
        {result.turnout_votes && <> ({result.turnout_votes.toLocaleString()} votes)</>}
        {result.majority_votes && <> · Majority {result.majority_votes.toLocaleString()}</>}
        {pct1(result.majority_pp) && <> ({pct1(result.majority_pp)}pp)</>}
        {result.rejected_ballots != null && <> · Rejected ballots {result.rejected_ballots}</>}
        {result.electorate && <> · Electorate {result.electorate.toLocaleString()}</>}
      </p>
      {result.grouping_note && <p class="caption">{result.grouping_note}</p>}
      {result.call_grade && (
        <p class="caption">
          <strong>How the call did.</strong> {result.call_grade.called}: {result.call_grade.verdict}.{" "}
          {result.call_grade.not_called_outcome} {result.call_grade.no_vote_share_forecast}
        </p>
      )}
    </section>
  )}

  {declared && result.candidates?.length > 0 && (
    <section class="section" id="candidate-results">
      <h2>Full candidate results</h2>
      <div class="by-election-scroll" role="region" aria-label="All 34 Clacton candidate results" tabindex="0"><table class="seat-table">
<caption>All 34 Clacton candidate results</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Candidate</th><th scope="col">Ballot description</th><th class="num" scope="col">Votes</th><th class="num" scope="col">Share</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{result.candidates.slice().sort((a:any,b:any)=>b.votes-a.votes).map((candidate:any)=>(
          <tr><td>{candidate.candidate}{candidate.candidate === result.winner_candidate && <strong> · Elected</strong>}</td><td>{candidate.description}</td><td class="num">{candidate.votes.toLocaleString("en-GB")}</td><td class="num">{pct1(candidate.votes/result.valid_votes)}%</td></tr>
        ))}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="caption">Shares are of {result.valid_votes.toLocaleString("en-GB")} valid votes. <a href="https://www.tendringdc.gov.uk/news/clacton-by-election-result-declared-after-record-breaking-election">Tendring's official result</a> lists all {result.candidates.length} candidates.</p>
    </section>
  )}

  <details class="section archived-analysis" id="archive" open={!declared}>
    <summary>Archived call, polling and pre-election analysis</summary>
    <p class="caption">The original analysis below is preserved for comparison. References to future outcomes describe what was known before the result; the declared counts appear above.</p>
  {showCall && (
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Our call · published at close of poll</p>
        <h2>{call.outcome}</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="backtest-callout">
        <strong>{call.headline}</strong>
        <span class="backtest-detail">{call.reasoning}</span>
      </div>
      <p class="caption" style="margin-top:var(--space-md)">
        Basis: {call.basis} · Confidence: {call.confidence}. This is a call on the shape of the
        ballot paper, not a vote-share forecast. What we are explicitly not calling:
      </p>
      <ul class="caption">
        {call.not_called.map((q: string) => <li>{q}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="caption">{call.standing_caveat}</p>
    </section>
  )}

  {cPolls.length > 0 && (
    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The only hard number</p>
        <h2>What the one constituency poll said</h2>
      </div>
      {cPolls.map((p: any) => (
        <>
          <PartyBars large={true} rows={pollRows(p)} />
          <p class="caption">
            {p.pollster} for {p.client}, fieldwork {p.fieldwork}, sample{" "}
            {p.sample_size}. Head to head, excluding the rest of the field:{" "}
            {shortPartyLabel("Reform UK")} {pct1(p.head_to_head["Reform UK"])}% against Count Binface{" "}
            {pct1(p.head_to_head["Count Binface Party"])}%.
            <a href={p.source} rel="external"> Source →</a>
          </p>
        </>
      ))}
      <p class="caption">{cPolling.note}</p>
      {exitPoll && exitPoll.exists === false && (
        <div class="backtest-callout" style="margin-top:var(--space-md)">
          <strong>There is no exit poll for this by-election.</strong>
          <span class="backtest-detail">{exitPoll.note}</span>
        </div>
      )}
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">{showCall ? "Method" : "Our call"}</p>
      <h2>Why there is no vote-share forecast</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="backtest-callout">
      <strong>{data.forecast.headline}</strong>
      <span class="backtest-detail">{data.forecast.no_forecast_rationale}</span>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      Method: qualitative · Provider: UK Elections. After Makerfield we publish a numeric central
      forecast only where a competitive race gives the models something real to estimate.
      <a href="/by-elections/makerfield/">What went wrong at Makerfield →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">How we got here</p>
      <h2>Timeline</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="by-election-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Clacton by-election timeline" tabindex="0"><table class="seat-table">
<caption>Clacton by-election timeline</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Event</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {timeline.map((t) => (
          <tr><td style="white-space:nowrap">{t.date}</td><td>{t.event}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="caption">
      {contest.trigger.stated_reason}
      {" "}{contest.precedent_note}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The ballot</p>
      <h2>Who stood, and who did not</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="backtest-callout">
      <strong>Five parties did not stand: {data.boycott.parties_not_standing.map((p: string) => shortPartyLabel(p)).join(", ")}.</strong>
      <span class="backtest-detail">{data.boycott.note}</span>
    </div>
    {data.field && (
      <p class="caption" style="margin-top:var(--space-md)">{data.field.record_note}</p>
    )}
    <div class="by-election-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Selected candidates in the original pre-election analysis" tabindex="0"><table class="seat-table" style="margin-top:var(--space-md)">
<caption>Selected candidates in the original pre-election analysis</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Candidate</th><th scope="col">Party</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.candidates.map((c: any) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{c.candidate}</td>
            <td>
              <span class="party-pill" style={`background:${partyColour(c.party)}`}></span>
              {c.party}
            </td>
            <td class="muted">{c.status}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="caption">
      The most notable of the {data.field ? data.field.candidate_count : ""} candidates on the ballot;
      nominations closed 17 July 2026. The balance are minor-party, novelty (several Monster Raving Loony)
      and independent candidates.{data.field?.correction_note ? <>{" "}{data.field.correction_note}</> : null}
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The baseline</p>
      <h2>Clacton at GE2024</h2>
    </div>
    <PartyBars
      large={true}
      rows={priorRows.map((r) => ({ party: r.party, pct: r.pct }))}
    />
    <p class="caption">
      4 July 2024: Nigel Farage (Reform UK) 21,225 votes (46.2%), majority 8,405 (18.3pp) over the
      Conservatives, on a turnout of 45,958 (58.7% of a 78,245 electorate). Clacton also elected
      Douglas Carswell for UKIP at the 2014 by-election, the party's first elected MP. With the
      2024 runners-up all absent from this ballot, the GE2024 shares are context, not a predictor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pre-election record</p>
      <h2>Questions recorded before polling</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      With no mainstream opposition, the winner call carries almost no information: John Curtice expects
      Farage to win comfortably in a seat that voted around 75% Leave. What we will record and grade when
      the result declares: <strong>turnout</strong> (the 2014 Carswell resign-and-recontest managed 51.0%,
      but that had all major parties standing, and this is mid-August); Farage's share against the 46.2% he
      won in a contested 2024 race; and <strong>second place</strong>, a genuine contest across a record
      field with no major party in it. An Ipsos "who do you want to win" question put Count Binface ahead
      of Farage, but that is a novelty measure of sentiment, not a turnout-weighted vote intention, and we
      are not treating it as a forecast.
    </p>
  </section>
  </details>
  <section class="section" id="sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p class="muted-row">
      Sources:
      {data.sources.map((s: any, i: number) => (
        <>
          {i > 0 ? " · " : " "}
          <a href={s.url} rel="external">{s.label}</a>
        </>
      ))}
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
